.TH V32IO 3v32 "October 14, 2026" "v32io 20261014-dev" "Vircon32 C Library"
.SH NAME
v32io \- keyboard and mouse for Vircon32 programs, through gamepad ports
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqkeyboard.h\(dq
.B #include \(dqmouse.h\(dq
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Vircon32 console has gamepads and nothing else to give input with. The
v32io project adds more devices by carrying their data on the 11 controls of
a gamepad: a program that knows the encoding reads a gamepad port and sees a
keyboard, or a mouse. This page is the overview of the C library a program
uses for that. It has three headers:
.TP
.BR v32io.h (3v32)
The core. Reads one gamepad and packs its 11 controls as the bits of an
integer; knows nothing of what they mean. Included by the other two.
.TP
.BR keyboard.h (3v32)
.B v32kbd
\(em a keyboard: key presses and releases by key code, typed text with shift
and caps lock applied, and typed characters outside ASCII (accented letters
and box drawing characters of the BIOS font).
.TP
.BR mouse.h (3v32)
.B v32mouse
\(em a mouse: a pointer with a position, the movement of each frame, and
three buttons.
.PP
Every function and structure has its own page that opens the page of the
header it belongs to, so
.B "man v32kbd_read"
and
.B "man v32mouse_probe"
work.
.SS The devices
.TP
.B Emulator
The Vircon32 DesktopEmulator with the v32io patches (in the project's
.I emulator
directory) has the devices
.B v32kbd
and
.B v32mouse
in its Gamepads menu, which use the keyboard and the mouse of the computer.
Select one for the gamepad port the program reads. The keyboard cannot be used
at the same time as the emulator's own keyboard gamepad.
.TP
.B Adapter
The RP2350\-USB\-A firmware (in the
.I firmware
directory) runs on a Raspberry Pi Pico 2 type board. A USB keyboard or mouse is
connected to it, and it presents itself to the computer that runs the
emulator as a USB gamepad. Real hardware, a different path to the same
program.
.PP
In both cases the program sees a gamepad. Nothing in the program says which
one it is.
.SS Programs
A program creates a device on a gamepad port, calls its
.B probe
function once on every frame, and then reads what happened:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#include "keyboard.h"

void main()
{
    v32kbd* keyboard = v32kbd_init( SECOND_GAMEPAD_PORT );
    int key = 0;

    while( true )
    {
        v32kbd_probe( &keyboard );
        key = v32kbd_read( &keyboard );
        while( key > 0 )
        {
            /* use the key */
            key = v32kbd_read( &keyboard );
        }
        end_frame();
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
There is nothing to link. The headers are installed beside the Vircon32
DevTools headers, so
.BR compile (1)
finds them with a plain
.B #include \(dqkeyboard.h\(dq
and no options, and it compiles only the functions that the program calls.
.SS Where things are
After the project is installed (see the
.I README
of the project):
.TP
.I Vircon32/DevTools/include
The headers, in the directory where the DevTools put theirs
.RI ( /usr/local/Vircon32/DevTools/include
on Linux and macOS, and
.I DevTools/include
under the install folder on Windows).
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/man/man3
These manual pages.
.TP
.I Vircon32/v32tools/docs/v32io
The documentation of the project, the protocol description and the example
programs.
.SS Version
.B V32IO_VERSION
and
.B V32IO_VERSION_NUMBER
(see
.BR v32io.h (3v32))
give the version of the headers a program was built with. The date and
version at the head of this page tell the version of the manual.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR v32io.h (3v32),
.BR keyboard.h (3v32),
.BR mouse.h (3v32),
.BR vircon32 (3v32),
.BR input.h (3v32),
.BR compile (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Matthew Haas, for the v32io project. The Vircon32 standard library it builds
on was written by Javier Carracedo
.RI ( Carra ).
